in @prism
in @kintsugi/fracture

# @kintsugi/fracture/generic-brackets — the first fracture rule.
#
# The drift: foreign-language angle-bracket parametric notation
# (Type<Param>) appearing in mirror source files. Training data is
# saturated with Rust/TypeScript/Java/C++ angle-bracket parametrics;
# the pull will reassert every time a new file gets written by
# someone whose fingers (or attention) remember a different language.
# Reed introduced this drift across ~10 boot files in the
# 2026-05-26 portal work; this fracture heals it and prevents
# recurrence at the substrate level.
#
# The canonical: mirror's own parametric notation Type(Param),
# already used throughout the boot tree (zoom(oid, gen_prism),
# imperfect(portal), etc.). The (...) form works at any arity per
# zoom(oid, gen_prism); there is no semantic reason for <> vs ()
# in mirror (no Foo() constructor-call ambiguity to disambiguate;
# types are first-class grammar declarations). Per Alex's 2026-05-26
# substrate-pull test: "if a 15-year engineer can't articulate the
# distinction, there isn't one." The <T> form is forty years of
# cargo-culted C++ parser accident with no purpose here.
#
# Per @kintsugi/fracture: this declaration follows the FP collapse
# — one function with two laws. Detection IS asking whether the
# function is a no-op on the input.

grammar @kintsugi/fracture/generic-brackets {
  # The fracture function. Walks the ast; rewrites every <T> in type
  # position to (T). Preserves all other bytes. Comparison/order
  # operators (<, <=, >, =) are NOT in type position and are left
  # untouched — the substrate's type-position check distinguishes
  # them structurally. The body is a Fate-resolved hole; the actual
  # rewrite logic lives at the downstream evaluator altitude. The
  # substrate-pull discipline says: declare the function + the laws;
  # the implementation is downstream.
  flow(ast) -> ast { \ }

  # Law 1: idempotent. Once <T> has been migrated to (T), running
  # flow again is a no-op — there are no further <T>s to find. The
  # canonical corpus is a fixed point. Bound via @kintsugi/fracture.
  requires idempotent(flow)

  # Law 2: canonical at fixpoint. flow(x) == x exactly when x has
  # no <T> drift. This IS the detection: a file is canonical iff
  # the fracture is a no-op on it. No separate detect operation.
  requires canonical_at_fixpoint(flow)
}

out flow
